  "body": "Maintainer, 2026-11-16: \"Issue, Bug, P2, mch, cps, themes, scripts - should be read from disk at runtme and not dompiled into the binary.\"\n\nWhat happens: the build compiles these data files into the executable with `include_bytes!` / `include_str!`. Known places:\n\n- the post processor scripts (`assets/posts/*.cps`), in `crates/app/src/posting.rs` (54 includes);\n- the machine files (`.mch`);\n- the themes;\n- the scripts.\n\nA user cannot change, add or replace one of these files without a new build. An executable without an `assets` directory beside it shows all of them.\n\nExpected: the application reads these files from disk at run time.\n\nWork:\n\n- List each `include_bytes!` / `include_str!` of a machine file, a post script, a theme or a script in `crates/*/src`. Icons, the application icon and fonts are not in the scope of this issue.\n- Define one data directory lookup (beside the executable first, then the per-user data directory) in one module. Each reader uses it.\n- Read the files at start or on first use, and cache the result. Do not read a file per frame.\n- A missing or malformed file gives a clear error that names the file and the directory. It does not crash. A post script that fails to load refuses the post by name.\n- Each release package ships the directories (see PR #2044, which ships `examples`). Packaging script and workflow changes need the maintainer's decision: name them in the PR body.\n- The browser build has no disk. Keep the compiled-in copy for the `wasm32` target only, and say so.\n- Tests read the files from the repository `assets` directory.\n\nSafety: a post script read from disk can be edited by a user, and it then changes the posted G-code. Show the path of the loaded post script in the post dialog or the program header comment, so that a user can see which file made the program. The verify check and the post gate do not change.\n\nOpen design questions (the worker records its choice in `docs/decisions/`):\n\n- Whether a compiled-in copy stays as a fallback on desktop when the directory is missing. The maintainer's words say no.\n- The order of the lookup when the same file name is in two directories.\n\nDone means: the desktop executable holds no compiled-in machine file, post script, theme or script. A test pins that an edited file on disk changes what the application loads. A test pins the error for a missing directory. The packages of a cut hold the directories.\n",
